<Project>

  <!--
    Repository-wide NuGet policy.

    Every other toolchain here states what it depends on and refuses to build
    on an advisory: Cargo has Cargo.lock plus `cargo audit`, Go has go.sum plus
    `govulncheck`, npm has package-lock.json plus `npm audit` and an allow-list.
    NuGet had none of the three, while `restore` and `build` execute MSBuild
    targets supplied by the packages themselves — so the one toolchain that
    runs third-party code at build time was also the one nothing in the
    pipeline could object to.

    MSBuild stops at the FIRST Directory.Build.props it finds walking up from a
    project, so this file governs a project only when no nearer one shadows it.
    `tests/unit/Directory.Build.props` therefore imports this file explicitly;
    without that import every test shard silently falls outside every rule
    below, which — together with a project weakening a property locally, or
    naming an NU19xx code in its own NoWarn — is the failure mode
    scripts/check-nuget-supply-chain.sh exists to refuse.

    ── The pinned graph ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Direct references are exact in the project files; transitives were not
    recorded anywhere, so two restores months apart could compile different
    code from the same commit. A lock file per project records the whole
    resolved graph with content hashes, and locked mode makes the graph a
    contract rather than a snapshot: restore fails instead of quietly
    resolving something else.

    ⚠️ Locked mode does NOT refuse a project that has no lock file — it writes
    one and exits 0. An absent lock file is therefore invisible here and is
    held by the guard instead, which reads the set of project files rather
    than the set of lock files.

    Adding or upgrading a package is expected to fail restore with NU1004,
    naming the reference that changed. `make nuget-relock` is the deliberate
    path through it.

    ── The advisory gate ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    NuGetAudit is on by default for direct references on this SDK and reports
    warnings. Both halves of that default are too weak to be a gate:

      * Transitive packages are where the graph actually is. Measured on this
        repository's own SDK: a reference whose own dependency carries three
        advisories reports 0 findings under `direct` and 3 under `all`.
      * A warning is not a refusal. NU1901-NU1904 (low → critical) are
        promoted to errors, matching the deny-warnings cargo-audit policy CI
        already runs for Rust.

    An advisory that must be carried is suppressed with a NuGetAuditSuppress
    item naming the advisory URL and the reason, next to the reference that
    pulls it — the same shape as .cargo/audit.toml, and for the same reason:
    a suppression nobody has to restate stops being a decision.

    NU1900 — the audit source did not answer — stays a warning on a developer
    machine, which may legitimately be offline, and becomes an error under CI,
    which may not. A scan that could not fetch advisory data reports zero
    findings and is indistinguishable from a clean one; that is the same trap
    an offline `npm audit` sets, and the only place it can be closed is where
    the network is guaranteed.
  -->

  <!--
    ── The pinned language ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    CI asks setup-dotnet for `8.0.x`, which resolves to whatever patch is
    newest on the day, and the compiler that ships inside a patch decides the
    default language version. That is a floating input: the same commit
    compiled on 8.0.420 and on 8.0.424 is compiled under two different
    languages, and only the second one is checked by anything.

    It is not hypothetical. A test declared `for (int field = 0; ...)` inside a
    property accessor, which is ordinary C# 12 and, from C# 14, a reference to
    the property's synthesized backing field — so the shard built clean on
    every developer machine and failed to compile in CI, on a patch bump
    nobody made. Pinning the version states which language this repository is
    written in, and moves the day that changes to a deliberate edit here.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>12.0</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1900</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
